msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: advanced.docbook:878
#, no-c-format
msgid "<literal>a.shortcut</literal> [optional]: The shortcut given here is the default shortcut. Example: Ctrl+Alt+t. See the <ulink url=\"http://doc.qt.nokia.com/latest/qt.html#Key-enum\">Qt documentation</ulink> for further details."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: advanced.docbook:883
#, no-c-format
msgid "Developing a command line script requires to reload the scripts to see whether the changes behave appropriately. Instead of restarting the application, simply switch to the command line and invoke the command <command>reload-scripts</command>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#. +> trunk stable
#: advanced.docbook:899
#, no-c-format
msgid "Scripting API"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: advanced.docbook:901
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The scripting API presented here is available in all scripts, i.e. indentation scripts and command line commands. Before the contents of a script is loaded, &kate; first adds several prototypes and functions to the scripting context. This convenience API contains prototypes like text cursors and text ranges and is located in the folder <filename>$KDEDIR/share/apps/katepart/api</filename>. There, the file names of each JavaScript file conforms with the following naming scheme: <programlisting>\n"
"&lt;number&gt;_&lt;name&gt;.js\n"
"</programlisting> The number has two digits and the name is an arbitrary name such as <quote><filename>myutils</filename></quote>. Through the number &kate; controls the execution order of the API. I.e., the file called <filename>05_cursor.js</filename> is called before the file <filename>10_range.js</filename>. (This is similar to the start order of the runlevel/init system in Linux)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: advanced.docbook:919
#, no-c-format
msgid "To extend the standard scripting API with own functions and prototypes simply create a new file in the KDE's local configuration folder <filename>$KDEHOME/share/apps/katepart/api</filename> and make sure it has a greater number (execution order) than the other <filename>*.js</filename> files in the global folder <filename>$KDEDIR/share/apps/katepart/api</filename>. &kate; automatically searches in both folders for <filename>*.js</filename> files and executes them in the correct order."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: advanced.docbook:929
#, no-c-format
msgid "To extend existing prototypes like <classname>Cursor</classname> or <classname>Range</classname>, the recommended way is to <emphasis>not</emphasis> modify the global <filename>*.js</filename> files. Instead, create a <filename>*.js</filename> file in the local folder that is being executed later than the <filename>05_cursor.js</filename> and then change the <classname>Cursor</classname> prototype in JavaScript."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#. +> trunk stable
#: advanced.docbook:939
#, no-c-format
msgid "Cursors and Ranges"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: advanced.docbook:941
#, no-c-format
msgid "As &kate; is a text editor, all the scripting API is based on cursors and ranges whenever possible. A Cursor is a simple <literal>(line, column)</literal> tuple representing a text position in the document. A Range spans text from a starting cursor position to an ending cursor position. The API is explained in detail in the next sections."
msgstr ""

